@page "/docs/get-started/from-mudblazor"

@* Moving an app from MudBlazor: running both side by side, setup, a component mapping, render modes, then
   buttons, fields and forms, data grids, dialogs, toasts, drag and drop and the app shell, each with
   MudBlazor's code beside IzzyBlazor's and the renamed members. MudBlazor names checked against its source
   (dev at 0a19a63, release 9.13.0, Nov 13 2026); IzzyBlazor's against its source. Both sides' samples were
   built against those versions. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="from-mudblazor">
    <Lead>
        Most MudBlazor components have an IzzyBlazor counterpart, so a page moves over by renaming components and members.
        The larger change is the render mode. MudBlazor requires every page that uses it to be interactive. IzzyBlazor
        works on static pages too: sorting, filters and paging are stored in the URL, and forms send a form post.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Running both libraries</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The two libraries can run side by side while you move page by page, as in
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/existing-app">Add to an existing app</Anchor>. A file with <Code>@@using</Code>
            for both gets ambiguous references, because these types have the same name in both: <Code>Color</Code>,
            <Code>Align</Code>, <Code>Justify</Code>, <Code>Orientation</Code>, <Code>Direction</Code>,
            <Code>Placement</Code>, <Code>Breakpoint</Code>, <Code>SelectionMode</Code>, <Code>Anchor</Code>,
            <Code>Shadow</Code>, <Code>DateRange</Code>, <Code>DialogService</Code>, <Code>DialogOptions</Code>,
            <Code>DialogResult</Code>, <Code>DataGridEditMode</Code>, <Code>FilterOperator</Code>,
            <Code>BreadcrumbItem</Code>, <Code>BreadcrumbSeparator</Code> and <Code>MenuContext</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            You'll hit <Code>Color</Code> first, because nearly every MudBlazor component accepts <Code>Color="Color.Primary"</Code>.
            <Code>PropertyColumn</Code> and <Code>TemplateColumn</Code> clash as tags (RZ9985), so a file that imports both
            libraries can't use either one's grid. Move a file to IzzyBlazor whole, or qualify the name
            (<Code>MudBlazor.Color.Primary</Code>).
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Setup</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Replace MudBlazor's services, stylesheet, font, script and providers. Add IzzyBlazor's theme to the head as
            <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme /&gt;</Code>. There's no script tag, because Blazor loads
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor> itself:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(SetupMud, SetupIzzy)
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Code>MudThemeProvider</Code> becomes <Code>IzzyTheme</Code>, with its <Code>Preset</Code>, <Code>Brand</Code> and fonts. It renders on the server, so the theme applies on the first paint. To let the user choose light or dark, add a <Code>ColorSchemeSwitch</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Remove <Code>MudPopoverProvider</Code>. Nothing replaces it, because menus, popovers and tooltips use the browser's popover support.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>MudDialogProvider</Code> becomes <Code>DialogHost</Code>, which must render interactively. In an interactive layout, as a MudBlazor app has, add it as it is. In a static layout, give it a render mode: <Code>&lt;DialogHost @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>MudSnackbarProvider</Code> becomes <Code>ToastRegion</Code>, which works in every render mode.</ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            Replace <Code>@@using MudBlazor</Code> in <Code>_Imports.razor</Code> with <Code>@@using IzzyBlazor</Code>, and remove
            any CSS you wrote against MudBlazor's <Code>mud-</Code> classes. For the rest of setup, see
            <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/by-hand">Set up by hand</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/theming/themes">Themes and presets</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Components</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each MudBlazor component below maps to the IzzyBlazor component that does the same job. IzzyBlazor's fields render
            their own label, hint and errors, and find their property from <Code>@@bind-Value</Code>, so there's no
            <Code>For</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        @* Two columns, the note under the IzzyBlazor name, so the notes get most of the width. *@
        <Table Items="Components" Caption="MudBlazor components and their IzzyBlazor counterparts" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-wrap" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="MudBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Mud)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">
                    @Rich(row.Izzy)
                    @if (row.Note.Length > 0)
                    {
                        <div class="site-param-text site-migrate-note">@Rich(row.Note)</div>
                    }
                </TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor> for every component, including ones MudBlazor doesn't have,
            such as <Code>Combobox</Code>, <Code>TreeList</Code>, <Code>Scheduler</Code> and <Code>RichTextEditor</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Render modes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            MudBlazor doesn't support static rendering. Its providers must render in the same interactive render mode as the
            components that use them. So a MudBlazor app is interactive on every page, or adds the providers to each
            interactive page.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's components work on <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">static pages</Anchor> too. An app that
            keeps every page interactive works as before. You can make pages static one at a time, and a static page doesn't
            keep a circuit open. Each component's Overview describes what it does in each render mode.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">State in the URL</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A MudBlazor grid stores its sort, page and filters in the component, and its <Code>ServerData</Code> reads them
            from a <Code>GridState</Code>. A <Code>Table</Code> or <Code>DataGrid</Code> stores them in the URL. A sortable
            header is a link that sets <Code>?sort=name</Code>, page numbers set <Code>?page=2</Code>, a filter sets
            <Code>?f-name=</Code>, and the search box sets <Code>?search=</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The view survives a reload and can be shared, and it works on static pages.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To keep the state in the component instead, bind it on interactive pages: <Code>@@bind-Sort</Code>,
            <Code>@@bind-Page</Code> and <Code>@@bind-Filter</Code>. <Code>Tabs</Code> and <Code>Pagination</Code> accept a
            <Code>QueryKey</Code> to store their state in the URL too. A dialog opens from <Code>?dialog=id</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Buttons</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>MudButton</Code> becomes <Code>Button</Code>, with <Code>Color</Code> as <Code>Accent</Code> and
            <Code>Variant</Code> as a <Code>ButtonVariant</Code>. The defaults differ. MudBlazor's default is a text
            button in the default color. IzzyBlazor's is a solid button in the primary accent, so a bare
            <Code>&lt;MudButton&gt;</Code> becomes <Code>Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost" Accent="Accent.Neutral"</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A button with only an icon is an <Code>IconButton</Code>, which requires a <Code>Label</Code>. Icons are
            Lucide's (<Code>Lucide.Save</Code>).
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(ButtonsMud, ButtonsIzzy)
        <Table Items="ButtonRenames" Caption="MudButton members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-pair" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="MudButton" Context="row">@Rich(row.Mud)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Button" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>OnClick</Code> runs on interactive pages. <Code>Href</Code>, <Code>Submit</Code> and <Code>Reset</Code> work
            everywhere. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fields and forms</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>EditForm</Code> with a <Code>DataAnnotationsValidator</Code>, or a <Code>MudForm</Code>, becomes
            <Code>Form</Code>, which adds the validator itself. On interactive pages, it validates as the user edits. On static
            pages, it sends a form post, even with JavaScript off. It then requires a <Code>FormName</Code> with a matching
            <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm]</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A field's label comes from <Code>[Display]</Code> or the property's name, and its required mark comes from
            <Code>[Required]</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(FormMud, FormIzzy)
        <Table Items="FieldRenames" Caption="MudBlazor field members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-pair" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="MudBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Mud)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            A field's value changes when the user leaves it, not on each keystroke. To update as the user types, as with
            <Code>Immediate</Code>, add an <Code>@@oninput</Code> handler. The field passes the handler to the input. See
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms">Forms and validation</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Data grids</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>MudDataGrid</Code> becomes a <Code>DataGrid</Code>, or a <Code>Table</Code> for a plain list of records, and
            <Code>MudTable</Code> becomes a <Code>Table</Code>. Columns stay in <Code>&lt;Columns&gt;</Code>, with the same
            <Code>PropertyColumn</Code> and <Code>TemplateColumn</Code> names. <Code>ServerData</Code> becomes an
            <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ToResultAsync</Code> applies the search, filters, sort and page to your query. It maps each column's key
            to its expression, so you don't need a switch on the sort column:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(GridMud, GridIzzy)
        <Table Items="GridRenames" Caption="MudDataGrid members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-pair" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="MudDataGrid" Context="row">@Rich(row.Mud)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="DataGrid and Table" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>What behaves differently:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Columns opt in.</strong> MudBlazor sorts every property column unless you turn it off. A column here sorts
                only with <Code>Sortable</Code> and filters only with <Code>Filterable</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A template column's content is the template</strong>, with <Code>context</Code> the item, not
                <Code>context.Item</Code>. To sort it, set <Code>SortBy</Code> to an expression, and set a
                <Code>SortKey</Code> for the URL.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>The selection is stored by key.</strong> With <Code>SelectionMode</Code> and an <Code>ItemKey</Code>,
                <Code>@@bind-Selection</Code> contains the rows' keys, so a selection survives paging and reloading.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components/data-grid">DataGrid</Anchor> and
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data">Loading data</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Dialogs</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Inject <Code>DialogService</Code> (a class, with no interface) in place of <Code>IDialogService</Code>.
            <Code>OpenAsync&lt;T&gt;</Code> accepts the title, the parameters by name and the options. It returns the
            <Code>DialogResult</Code> itself, so there's no dialog reference to await:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(OpenMud, OpenIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <TextBlock>
            Inside the dialog, the cascaded <Code>DialogInstance</Code> replaces <Code>IMudDialogInstance</Code>. There are no
            <Code>DialogContent</Code> or <Code>DialogActions</Code> slots. The content goes directly in the dialog, and a
            <Code>DialogFooter</Code> written last contains the buttons:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(InsideMud, InsideIzzy)
        <Table Items="DialogRenames" Caption="IDialogService members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-pair" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="MudBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Mud)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Close with the data itself.</strong> Call <Code>Dialog.Close(server.Id)</Code>, not
                <Code>Close(DialogResult.Ok(server.Id))</Code>. The second still compiles, but the caller's <Code>Data</Code>
                is then a <Code>DialogResult</Code>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>A confirm puts the message first.</strong> <Code>ConfirmAsync(message, title)</Code> is the reverse of
                <Code>ShowMessageBoxAsync(title, message)</Code>, and it returns a <Code>bool</Code>. It opens with focus on Cancel,
                and Escape cancels.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        @SideBySide(ConfirmMud, ConfirmIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <TextBlock>
            A dialog written in the page opens with <Code>@@bind-Open</Code>, a <Code>DialogTrigger</Code> or a link to
            <Code>?dialog=id</Code>. The last two work on static pages. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/dialog">Dialog</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Toasts</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>ISnackbar</Code> becomes <Code>ToastService</Code>, and its toasts appear in the layout's
            <Code>ToastRegion</Code>. Call <Code>Success</Code>, <Code>Info</Code>, <Code>Warning</Code> or <Code>Error</Code>
            with the message and an optional title:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(ToastMud, ToastIzzy, language: "csharp")
        <TextBlock>
            Toasts work in every render mode. A toast sent while a static page renders appears in that page, and one sent
            before a redirect appears on the next page. An action with an <Code>Href</Code> is a link, so it works on static
            pages too.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Error toasts stay until the user closes them. To keep any toast open, set
            <Code>Duration: TimeSpan.Zero</Code>, as with <Code>RequireInteraction</Code>. The region's
            <Code>Placement</Code> and <Code>Duration</Code> replace the snackbar configuration. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/toast-region">ToastRegion</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Drag and drop</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A <Code>MudDropContainer</Code> with <Code>MudDropZone</Code>s becomes a <Code>DropZoneGroup</Code> with a
            <Code>ListView</Code> per zone. As in MudBlazor, the group contains one list of items and sets which zone each item is in:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(DropMud, DropIzzy)
        <Table Items="DropRenames" Caption="MudDropContainer members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-pair" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="MudBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Mud)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Each item's handle opens a menu with the same moves. Moving works with the keyboard, on touch and with JavaScript
            off. With JavaScript off, <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>SubmitOnMove</Code> post the moves. Set <Code>TItem</Code>
            on the group when it has an <Code>OnMove</Code> handler. See <Anchor Href="docs/components/drop-zone-group">DropZoneGroup</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The app shell</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>MudLayout</Code>, <Code>MudAppBar</Code>, <Code>MudDrawer</Code> and <Code>MudMainContent</Code> become
            <Code>AppShell</Code>, <Code>AppHeader</Code>, <Code>AppSidebar</Code> and <Code>AppMain</Code>. A
            <Code>SidebarToggle</Code> opens the sidebar on small screens without any state in your code, and it works with
            JavaScript off. Style props, such as <Code>Padding</Code>, replace utility classes such as <Code>pa-4</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        @SideBySide(ShellMud, ShellIzzy)
        <Table Items="ShellRenames" Caption="MudLayout members" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-pair" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="MudBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Mud)</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="IzzyBlazor" Context="row">@Rich(row.Izzy)</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            See <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: Combobox, TreeList, Scheduler and the rest of what you may have built by hand.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what changes when some pages become static.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/theming/tokens">Tokens and customizing</Anchor>: CSS variables for your own styles beside the components.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    // MudBlazor's code on the left, IzzyBlazor's on the right; one under the other until the content is
    // wide enough for both.
    private static RenderFragment SideBySide(string before, string after, string language = "razor") =>
        @<Grid MinChildWidth="Length.Rem(26)" Gap="3">
            <Code Block Language="@language" Title="MudBlazor">@before</Code>
            <Code Block Language="@language" Title="IzzyBlazor">@after</Code>
        </Grid>;

    private sealed record Mapping(string Mud, string Izzy, string Note = "");

    private static Mapping Map(string mud, string izzy, string note = "") => new(mud, izzy, note);

    // A table cell: text, with the parts between backticks as code.
    private static RenderFragment Rich(string text) => builder =>
    {
        var parts = text.Split('`');
        for (var i = 0; i < parts.Length; i++)
        {
            if (i % 2 == 1)
            {
                builder.OpenElement(0, "code");
                builder.AddContent(1, parts[i]);
                builder.CloseElement();
            }
            else
            {
                builder.AddContent(2, parts[i]);
            }
        }
    };

    private static readonly Mapping[] Components =
    [
        // Buttons
        Map("`MudButton`", "`Button`", "`Color` is `Accent`, and `Variant` is a `ButtonVariant`. See Buttons below."),
        Map("`MudIconButton`", "`IconButton`", "Requires a `Label`. For MudBlazor's look, add `Variant=\"ButtonVariant.Ghost\" Accent=\"Accent.Neutral\"`."),
        Map("`MudFab`", "`FloatingButton`", "Fixed to a corner of the window. An inline round button has no equivalent yet."),
        Map("`MudFabMenu`", "`SpeedDial`", "Its actions are `SpeedDialAction`s."),
        Map("`MudToggleIconButton`", "`ToggleButton`", "`@bind-Toggled` is `@bind-Value`. It accepts `Icon`, `CheckedIcon` and `IconOnly`."),
        Map("`MudButtonGroup`", "`ButtonGroup`", "`Vertical` is `Orientation`."),
        Map("`MudToggleGroup`", "`SegmentedControl`, or a `CheckboxGroup` with `Chips`", "For one value, use a `SegmentedControl` with `Items`. For several, use a `CheckboxGroup` with `Chips`."),
        Map("`MudToolBar`", "`Toolbar`", "What doesn't fit moves into a menu."),

        // Fields
        Map("`MudTextField`", "`TextField`, `TextArea`, `PasswordField`", "For `Lines` above one, use a `TextArea`. For `InputType.Password`, use a `PasswordField`. `HelperText` is `Hint`, and `Adornment` is `StartIcon` or `EndIcon`."),
        Map("`MudNumericField`", "`NumberField`", "`Min`, `Max` and `Step` work as before. `HideSpinButtons` is `StepButtons`."),
        Map("`MudSelect`, `MudSelectItem`", "`Select`, or `MultiSelect`", "Pass `Items` and `ItemText` instead of item children. The items are the values. To filter as the user types, use `Combobox`."),
        Map("`MudAutocomplete`", "`Combobox`, or `Autocomplete` for free text", "`SearchFunc` is `Search`, with the same shape. `ToStringFunc` is `ItemText`."),
        Map("`MudDatePicker`", "`DateField`", "`@bind-Date` is `@bind-Value`, to a `DateOnly`, `DateTime` or `DateTimeOffset`. `MinDate` and `MaxDate` are `Min` and `Max`. For a calendar on the page, use `Calendar`."),
        Map("`MudDateRangePicker`", "`DateRangeField`", "Binds an `IzzyBlazor.DateRange` of `DateOnly?`."),
        Map("`MudTimePicker`", "`TimeField`", "`@bind-Time` (`TimeSpan?`) is `@bind-Value` to a `TimeOnly`. For a length of time, use `DurationField`."),
        Map("`MudCheckBox`", "`Checkbox`", "Binds a `bool`. `TriState` is `Indeterminate`."),
        Map("`MudSwitch`", "`Switch`"),
        Map("`MudRadioGroup`, `MudRadio`", "`RadioGroup`", "Pass `Items` and `ItemText` instead of radio children. `Chips` shows them as chips."),
        Map("`MudChipSet` (choosing)", "`RadioGroup` or `CheckboxGroup` with `Chips`"),
        Map("`MudSlider`", "`Slider`, or `RangeSlider` for a range", "`Vertical` is `Orientation`, and `ValueLabel` is `ValueTooltip`."),
        Map("`MudRating`", "`Rating`", "`@bind-SelectedValue` is `@bind-Value`, and `MaxValue` is `Max`."),
        Map("`MudColorPicker`", "`ColorPicker`, or `ColorPanel` on the page", "The value is a hex string, not a `MudColor`."),
        Map("`MudFileUpload`", "`FileUpload`", "`FilesChanged` is `OnChange`, and `MaximumFileCount` is `MaxFiles`. On static pages it posts with a `Form` set to `Multipart`."),
        Map("`MudForm`, `EditForm`", "`Form`", "See Fields and forms below."),
        Map("`ValidationSummary`", "`FormErrors`", "Moves focus to itself after a failed submit."),
        Map("`MudField`", "`FieldFrame`", "Renders the label, hint and errors for a control of your own."),
        Map("`MudExitPrompt`", "`Form` with `ConfirmLeave`"),

        // Data
        Map("`MudDataGrid`", "`DataGrid`, or `Table`", "The sort, filters, page and search are stored in the URL. See Data grids below."),
        Map("`MudTable`", "`Table`", "Uses columns instead of `RowTemplate` cells. `ServerData` is `ItemsProvider`, and `OnRowClick` is `RowHref`."),
        Map("`MudSimpleTable`", "A `<table>` in `Prose`"),
        Map("`MudList`", "`List`, or `ListView` for items to select or reorder"),
        Map("`MudTreeView`", "`TreeView`", "Accepts `Items`, `ItemChildren` and `ItemText`. `ServerData` is `ChildrenProvider`."),
        Map("`MudTimeline`", "`Timeline`"),
        Map("`MudDropContainer`, `MudDropZone`", "`DropZoneGroup`, with a `ListView` per zone", "See Drag and drop below."),
        Map("`MudCarousel`", "`Carousel`", "`AutoCycle` is `Autoplay`, `ShowArrows` is `Arrows`, `ShowBullets` is `Dots`, and `SelectedIndex` is `Index`."),
        Map("`MudChart`", "`Chart`, `PieChart`, `HeatmapChart`, `RadarChart`, `SankeyChart`", "A chart accepts `Items` with `Argument` and `Value` expressions, not arrays of series. The rose chart has no equivalent yet."),
        Map("`MudPagination`", "`Pagination`", "`Count` is `PageCount`, and `@bind-Selected` is `@bind-Page`, or `QueryKey` for the URL."),

        // Overlays and feedback
        Map("`IDialogService`, `MudDialog`", "`DialogService`, `Dialog`", "Add a `DialogHost` to the layout. See Dialogs below."),
        Map("`ShowMessageBoxAsync`, `MudMessageBox`", "`ConfirmAsync`, or `ConfirmButton` on static pages", "The message comes first. A three-way message box has no equivalent yet."),
        Map("`ISnackbar`", "`ToastService`", "Add a `ToastRegion` to the layout. See Toasts below."),
        Map("`MudPopover`", "`Popover`", "Has its own trigger: set a `Label`, or pass a `Trigger` of your own."),
        Map("`MudMenu`, `MudMenuItem`", "`Menu`, `MenuItem`", "`ActivatorContent` is `Trigger`, and a nested `MudMenu` is a `MenuSub`. For a right-click menu, use `ContextMenu`."),
        Map("`MudTooltip`", "`Tooltip`", "Accepts `Text` and `Placement`. `Placement` is a different enum with the same name."),
        Map("`MudDrawer` as a side panel", "`Drawer`", "A dialog that slides in from an edge."),
        Map("`MudAlert`", "`Alert`, or `Banner` for the whole app", "`Severity` is `Status`, and `ShowCloseIcon` is `Dismissible`."),
        Map("`MudProgressLinear`", "`Progress`", "Leave `Value` out for an indeterminate bar. `Buffer` has no equivalent yet."),
        Map("`MudProgressCircular`", "`ProgressCircle`, or `Spinner` with no value"),
        Map("`MudSkeleton`", "`Skeleton`"),
        Map("`MudBadge`", "`Badge`", "An inline label, or `Badge` on a `NavItem` or `Tab`. A badge over a child has no equivalent yet."),
        Map("`MudChip` (showing)", "`Tag`", "`OnClose` is `OnRemove`, or `RemoveHref` on static pages."),

        // Navigation and layout
